/**
 * @defgroup DspCore_Interpolation Interpolation and lookup
 * @brief Float/double interpolation arithmetic and explicit LUT boundaries.
 *
 * Include `<DspCore_Interpolation.h>` or
 * `<DspCore/Interpolation/Arithmetic.h>` for standalone formulas. LUT lookup
 * additionally requires the LUT module and `<DspCore/Interpolation/Lookup.h>`.
 * No operation allocates, throws or modifies table storage.
 *
 * @section DspCore_Interpolation_Formulas Arithmetic
 *
 * Linear(a,b,t) evaluates a line with normalized t; Bilinear and Trilinear
 * compose it on independent normalized axes. Values outside [0,1] extrapolate.
 * No boundary policy is hidden in the formulas. Use consistent float or double
 * arguments (or an explicit template argument); integer-only arithmetic is
 * rejected at compile time. Double computations are never reduced to float.
 *
 * Corner order is x-fast, matching LUT storage:
 * - Bilinear: f00, f10, f01, f11, x, y.
 * - Trilinear: f000, f100, f010, f110, f001, f101, f011, f111, x, y, z.
 *
 * `f(x,y) = L(L(f00,f10,x), L(f01,f11,x), y)`
 *
 * Linear returns exact endpoints at t=0 and t=1 and uses a weighted form
 * for opposite-sign endpoints to avoid overflowing their difference during
 * interpolation. NaN/Inf samples may propagate; extrapolation may overflow.
 * Results are not clipped. Exact endpoints select the corresponding sample
 * even if an unused sample is non-finite.
 *
 * @section DspCore_Interpolation_Coordinates Physical coordinates
 *
 * TryUnitCoordinate(x,low,high,t) validates finite coordinates and ascending
 * bounds. TryLinear/TryBilinear/TryTrilinear normalize physical coordinates
 * before applying the formulas. Each span, subtraction and normalized result
 * must be representable. Reversed or collapsed bounds fail rather than divide
 * by zero. Failure leaves the output unchanged. Extrapolation is permitted.
 * The bool reports coordinate validity, not finiteness of sample arithmetic.
 *
 * @code{.cpp}
 * double value = 0;
 * // Physical x=15 in [10,20], output endpoints 2 and 8: value=5.
 * bool ok = DspCore::Interpolation::TryLinear(10.,20.,2.,8.,15.,value);
 * @endcode
 *
 * @section DspCore_Interpolation_Lookup Normalized LUT lookup
 *
 * Lookup(table, coordinates..., output, policy) returns bool and takes an
 * explicit Boundary policy shared across axes:
 *
 * | Boundary | Domain and indexing |
 * | --- | --- |
 * | Clamp | Clamp to [0,1]; position=x*(N-1), with exact first/last endpoints |
 * | Reject | Same closed indexing, but outside [0,1] returns false |
 * | Wrap | Wrap cycles into [0,1); position=x*N, interpolating last-to-first |
 *
 * Wrap tables contain N distinct periodic samples without a duplicate
 * endpoint. Thus a four-sample table has sample 1 at phase 0.25. Closed
 * tables include both endpoints; their sample 1 is at coordinate 1/3.
 * Negative phase and arbitrarily many finite cycles wrap with fmod.
 *
 * Empty tables, invalid policies and non-finite coordinates fail without
 * changing output. Singleton axes are constant for valid coordinates and
 * still obey Reject; no division by zero occurs. Interior indexing requires
 * the extent to fit the exact-integer range of the calculation type and be
 * smaller than size_t's maximum, avoiding unsafe floating-to-index casts.
 *
 * The floating output/coordinate type selects arithmetic independently of
 * table storage. Integer samples convert to that type without PCM scaling.
 * Narrowing sample overflow explicitly produces signed infinity; NaN remains
 * NaN. Lookup success reports valid indexing, not finite sample values.
 * Select double coordinates and output to preserve double sample precision.
 *
 * @code{.cpp}
 * DspCore::LUT::LUT1D<float> table;
 * if (!table.Resize(2)) { return; }
 * table.SetValue(0, 0.0f); table.SetValue(1, 10.0f);
 * float value = 0;
 * bool ok = DspCore::Interpolation::Lookup(
 *     table, 0.25f, value, DspCore::Interpolation::Boundary::Clamp);
 * // value=2.5; no allocation during lookup.
 * @endcode
 *
 * @section DspCore_Interpolation_Examples Examples
 *
 * Interpolation/Linear: physical coordinates, extrapolation, invalid spans.
 * Interpolation/Lookup: closed versus periodic indexing, negative cycles,
 * bilinear and trilinear fields. Shared Arduino/desktop code prints actual
 * and expected values and PASS/FAIL.
 *
 * See @ref DspCore_LUT and SPEC-DOM-1 in @ref DspCore_Numeric.
 */
